                <pb facs="0812" n="700" xml:id="pg700"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13330: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-12-31T13:00:00+08:00">December 31, 1935—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 31—6:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>250. Following telegram has been received from the Consul General at Hankow:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“December 30, 5 p.m. My despatch No. 209, December 14th. One or two
                            groups of Reds which were penetrating southwest Hunan toward Kweichow
                            have apparently encountered resistance on the border and are moving
                            north. Catholic mission Chihkiang telegraphed December 28th that Reds
                            were fighting in the Hungkiang and Kienyang area, and that the situation
                            at Chihkiang was very tense. I telegraphed December 29th to the
                            Provincial Government at Changsha asking for protection of missionaries
                            at Chihkiang of whom there are nine Americans. Telegram from Yuanling
                            December 29th received today states heavy troop movements from there
                            toward Chihkiang and the authorities assure safety Chihkiang.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
